WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:02.702
All right, he's the comic
whose latest book is,

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:06.239
<i>Hey Mom: Stories for My Mother,
But You Can Read Them Too,</i>

00:00:06.473 --> 00:00:09.175
whose new comedy special
<i>Big Underwear</i> is on Amazon,

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:11.510
who is the Emmy-winning star
of<i> Baskets,</i>

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:12.711
take a look.

00:00:12.945 --> 00:00:14.947
I don't wanna look bad on TV.

00:00:15.114 --> 00:00:16.115
KEN:<i> You won't.</i>

00:00:16.181 --> 00:00:18.317
-I'm a rodeo-owner. (CHUCKLES)
<i> -I know.</i>

00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:19.552
Can you believe it?

00:00:19.885 --> 00:00:22.755
<i> Christine,
why don't you just go out
and buy something new, honey?</i>

00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:25.391
I've already tried everything on
at The Dress Barn.

00:00:25.524 --> 00:00:27.593
-(KEN SIGHS)
-I'm in between sizes.

00:00:27.794 --> 00:00:28.994
(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:00:29.161 --> 00:00:32.231
Well, please welcome
my old friend Louie Anderson!

00:00:32.364 --> 00:00:36.469
(CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)

00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:38.905
Louie, it's been too long!

00:00:39.071 --> 00:00:40.473
-I love you, Bill.
-BILL: I love you too.

00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:42.275
-How are you?
-Hi, everybody!

00:00:42.341 --> 00:00:44.076
BILL: Louie Anderson.

00:00:44.143 --> 00:00:46.679
-(CHEERING CONTINUES)
-Thank you so much.

00:00:47.212 --> 00:00:49.582
-(WHISTLING)
-Thank you!

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:53.085
-Louie...
-I do not want Trump
messing with Amazon

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:55.688
till my book is off
the Best Seller list.

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:56.756
(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:00:56.822 --> 00:00:59.659
-Please, Donald. Please.
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:02.395
Well, Louie, it's been
such an argumentative show.

00:01:02.495 --> 00:01:04.364
-It has been.
But it's been good.
-And I--

00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:07.099
-Oh, well...
-I loved the piece with Geraldo.

00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:09.369
'Cause I love that you stuck
to your guns,

00:01:09.636 --> 00:01:13.673
-and I love that you still are--
You still like Geraldo.
-(APPLAUSE)

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.274
-Yeah, absolutely. Well--
-Yeah.

00:01:15.407 --> 00:01:17.210
-And I think we have to not--
-You know who else--

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:20.145
-Who I like is Roseanne!
-I love Roseanne. I--

00:01:20.279 --> 00:01:21.847
And congratulations, Roseanne!

00:01:21.948 --> 00:01:23.983
-Yes, congratulations. Yes.
-We can say that.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:25.551
-You know, people got shit.
-I mean--

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:28.354
My friend Sarah Silverman
got shit for congratulating
Roseanne,

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:30.523
-it's ridiculous
that we can't even--
-And it's crazy.

00:01:30.656 --> 00:01:32.158
And Roseanne is crazy.

00:01:32.225 --> 00:01:34.059
Yes, but that's beside
the point.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.362
-Beside the point, exactly.
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:37.663
(BILL LAUGHS)

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:40.800
Everybody has someone crazy
in their family.

00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:44.671
-Sometimes it's you.
-Yes! (CHUCKLES) Well. Oh. Oh.

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:46.705
-No, I mean you're--
-No, I get it. I get it.

00:01:46.772 --> 00:01:47.774
(BILL LAUGHS)

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:50.375
(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:01:50.509 --> 00:01:52.177
I get it.
I'm so happy to be here.

00:01:52.311 --> 00:01:54.480
I miss you.
I haven't seen you in so long.

00:01:54.614 --> 00:01:57.282
-I know.
-We know each other
a very, very long time.

00:01:57.416 --> 00:01:59.652
Since the early, early days
of The Comedy Store,

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:01.420
when we were gonna write that...

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:03.088
-It wasn't--
-...that sitcom or something,

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:04.457
-weren't we?
-Yes, we did. We--

00:02:04.524 --> 00:02:06.225
Was it<i> Brothers,</i>
or<i> Buddies,</i> or...?

00:02:06.392 --> 00:02:09.228
We did, we tried to write
a sitcom together once.

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:11.097
You were gonna be
a conservative.

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:13.465
-No.
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:15.334
-(BILL CHUCKLES)
-I'm sorry.

00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:18.371
-Cheap joke.
-But, um, look,
congratulations on every--

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:20.006
-I mean, you're always working,
I swear.
-Thank you.

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:23.042
And you do so many
different things, you know,
you were a gameshow host,

00:02:23.175 --> 00:02:25.178
and now you've got
this brilliant,
Emmy-winning performance,

00:02:25.245 --> 00:02:27.313
-and that is your mother
you're doing, right?
-Yes.

00:02:27.446 --> 00:02:29.215
-And that--
-That is Ora Zella Anderson.

00:02:29.381 --> 00:02:30.382
-(AUDIENCE CHEERS)
-Okay.

00:02:30.616 --> 00:02:31.817
-Love you, Mom.
-(APPLAUSE)

00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:34.053
And the book is
about your mother, I mean,

00:02:34.253 --> 00:02:36.889
she's kinda your hero, because
she-- she helped to step in

00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:39.525
between you and your-- your
bad father, right? Bad father.

00:02:39.725 --> 00:02:42.128
Yeah, yeah. You know, drunken,
you know, so...

00:02:42.261 --> 00:02:44.430
-I forgive him, but drunken.
-Yes. Yeah.

00:02:44.563 --> 00:02:49.335
And, you know, 11 children,
and she stood
between him and us, and...

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:50.636
-BILL: Right.
-...she saved us.

00:02:50.736 --> 00:02:52.571
I mean, she--
she deflected, and--

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:55.508
and protected, and--
and then also fed us,

00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:58.243
which, uh... really was,
you know,

00:02:58.377 --> 00:03:01.647
-why I'm so fat, is because--
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:03:01.847 --> 00:03:04.016
-Well, no, it's true though.
-I wasn't gonna mention it.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.220
Well... (CHUCKLES)
It's the elephant in the room.

00:03:07.386 --> 00:03:08.788
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)
-Um...

00:03:09.054 --> 00:03:11.824
-It's not...
-(APPLAUSE)

00:03:12.358 --> 00:03:13.459
Oh.

00:03:14.127 --> 00:03:17.763
Uh... No, but you know,
I don't mean to say that
she was bad by feeding us,

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.532 line:0%
-but when things were rough...
-BILL: Right.

00:03:20.866 --> 00:03:23.736 line:0%
-...afterwards,
"Here, have some soup."
-BILL: Comfort food?

00:03:23.836 --> 00:03:25.404 line:0%
-Yeah, comfort food. Yeah.
-BILL: Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:25.504 --> 00:03:27.139 line:0%
I miss her, I love her, and, uh,

00:03:27.306 --> 00:03:30.376
she-- she really has made
this character come to life.

00:03:30.443 --> 00:03:32.311
I'm so happy she has
a starring role on TV.

00:03:32.411 --> 00:03:35.415
Yeah. And playing--
A man playing a woman,

00:03:35.582 --> 00:03:38.884
-has anybody given you any shit
for taking a job from a woman?
-No.

00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:40.419
(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:03:40.686 --> 00:03:42.321
'Cause-- No, that goes on
all the time now.

00:03:42.488 --> 00:03:43.756
Yeah, yeah. No, I--

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.091
-You dare not play--
-I never thought of--

00:03:45.224 --> 00:03:48.193
Until you said that,
I really had never
thought of it,

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:51.864
-because I am--
-BILL: Oh, oh. I don't wanna
start trouble.

00:03:51.998 --> 00:03:54.367
Well, I don't mind, I don't...
I just think that

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:57.403
I agree, I understand that thing
but this is a comedy.

00:03:57.737 --> 00:03:58.804
-BILL: Right.
-We're not--

00:03:59.338 --> 00:04:02.107
-You know, that we're trying
to make a comedy, and I would--
-I know, I-- Okay.

00:04:02.307 --> 00:04:05.111
If I was just a guy there,
it would be terrible playing.

00:04:05.211 --> 00:04:06.712
-(CHUCKLES)
-But I know what you mean. I--

00:04:06.846 --> 00:04:09.148
-Do not try to explain logic...
-Yeah.

00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:10.716
-Yeah, you're right.
-...to the purity police.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:12.051
-Yes, I won't.
-They just wanna be mad.

00:04:12.118 --> 00:04:13.386
-Yes, that's true.
-They just--

00:04:13.452 --> 00:04:16.255
They wanna be mad.
Why doesn't a woman
get that part?

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:19.659
-(CHUCKLES)
-Because that woman is not
funny, so she can't have it,

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:21.694
-the woman who's gonna play it.
-Right. Right.
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:23.296
-You know, can I just say this?
-You could--

00:04:23.496 --> 00:04:26.098
Uh... Zach wanted a woman.
He had her picked out.

00:04:26.265 --> 00:04:30.503
Brenda was a British actress,
I never can remember her name,
I should call and thank her,

00:04:30.737 --> 00:04:34.140
but, um, she was busy,
and she couldn't do the part.

00:04:34.674 --> 00:04:37.743
And then Zach said,
"I have an idea of a voice,"

00:04:37.976 --> 00:04:39.178
and he said the voice,

00:04:39.312 --> 00:04:41.447
and somebody said,
"It sounds like Louie Anderson."

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:44.717
"Aah," Zach went like that,
and he went, "Call him,"

00:04:44.883 --> 00:04:47.152
and they called me,
and that's really
how I got the part.

00:04:47.619 --> 00:04:49.187
Screw Judi Dench, huh?

00:04:49.288 --> 00:04:51.290
-(CHUCKLES) Yeah.
-(APPLAUSE)

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:52.625
Um...

00:04:54.494 --> 00:04:56.762
So, uh, I read that
you cry every day.

00:04:56.929 --> 00:04:58.564
Yeah. I-- I was...

00:04:58.698 --> 00:05:01.567
-Tell me about that,
'cause I-- I am not a cryer.
-Um...

00:05:01.633 --> 00:05:03.069
You know when I cry?
I cry at movies.

00:05:03.402 --> 00:05:05.604
-Yes.
-It's so easy to get me
to cry at a movie,

00:05:05.738 --> 00:05:08.440
and so hard in life.
What does that say about me?

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:10.109
-Um...
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:05:10.209 --> 00:05:11.510
-Let's talk about me, Louie.
-I think--

00:05:11.677 --> 00:05:13.879
Yeah, I would guess that
that would say about you is

00:05:14.079 --> 00:05:16.615
that you're extremely
vulnerable.

00:05:16.782 --> 00:05:18.617
-I mean, that you're--
-To movies.

00:05:18.817 --> 00:05:20.920
-No, to...
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:05:21.187 --> 00:05:22.854
No, but that you're
a vulnerable person...

00:05:23.021 --> 00:05:24.923
-Oh.
-...and crying would leave you--

00:05:25.123 --> 00:05:28.527
'Cause you're, you know,
you would be wide open
on that front,

00:05:28.628 --> 00:05:30.429
and that could be where--
Maybe that's where--

00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:33.031
But how do you--
How do you get yourself
to cry every day?

00:05:33.131 --> 00:05:34.133
Um...

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:36.101
You just think
of something sad?

00:05:36.635 --> 00:05:40.105
No, I'll be talking, you know,
like, on the book tour
I've talked about my brother

00:05:40.239 --> 00:05:42.574
-who is no longer with us...
-BILL: Okay, okay.

00:05:42.641 --> 00:05:44.877
-...and, you know, I just,
I miss him. I miss him.
-BILL: That's sad.

00:05:44.943 --> 00:05:46.112
I miss him, it's sad.

00:05:46.245 --> 00:05:50.148
And also, you know we talk about
everything that's going on in--

00:05:50.282 --> 00:05:53.218
Right now, the girl who shot up
the YouTube thing?

00:05:53.418 --> 00:05:54.687
-BILL: Right.
-You know, we, as--

00:05:54.787 --> 00:05:56.622
You know, I have mental illness
in my family, and...

00:05:56.755 --> 00:06:00.092
-BILL: Right.
-We used to have safeguards
for mental illness.

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:03.963
There used to be hospitals
and places and checking on,

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.732
in a neighborhood,
when things went bad,
we knew the police,

00:06:07.032 --> 00:06:10.035
we were connected,
we have stopped being connected

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:11.470
-as human beings.
-That's so true.

00:06:11.603 --> 00:06:13.873
-We are not connected.
-(APPLAUSE)

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:15.308
And if my...

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:19.946 line:0%
If our neighbors were doing
something across the street...

00:06:20.446 --> 00:06:23.115 line:0%
-BILL: Right.
-...my dad went,
"What the-- Huh?"

00:06:23.248 --> 00:06:26.385 line:0%
-(BILL LAUGHS)
-Honestly. "What are they doing
over there?"

00:06:26.485 --> 00:06:27.553
(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:06:28.087 --> 00:06:30.889
And he would call someone
who'd go over there,
"What are you doing over here?"

00:06:30.989 --> 00:06:31.991
Yeah.

00:06:32.391 --> 00:06:34.960
"You can't have 100 guns
in here."

00:06:35.127 --> 00:06:37.830
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)
-But we-- we...

00:06:38.864 --> 00:06:40.566
(BILL STUTTERS, CHUCKLES)

00:06:40.666 --> 00:06:41.767
I don't mean you should do that.

00:06:41.867 --> 00:06:42.868
I don't--

00:06:42.935 --> 00:06:44.002
We don't want that either,
right?

00:06:44.136 --> 00:06:45.771
-We don't want people--
-Well, no, but you want this.

00:06:45.904 --> 00:06:48.040
-But you want--
-You want a little of that,
but not too--

00:06:48.174 --> 00:06:50.742
You want your mom going,
"Well, I'll call Shirley,

00:06:50.876 --> 00:06:53.279
-'cause she knows the sheriff,
and she'll--"
-BILL: Right, exactly.

00:06:53.379 --> 00:06:55.147
-BILL: Well--
-We were connected,
we knew the police.

00:06:55.214 --> 00:06:57.683
There was a-- there was
a book once, called
<i> It Takes a Village...</i>

00:06:57.850 --> 00:06:59.585
-Yes.
-...and that's what
that was about.

00:06:59.651 --> 00:07:00.652
-Yes.
-Of course.

00:07:00.719 --> 00:07:02.387
-That's a wonderful book.
-It was.

00:07:02.454 --> 00:07:03.622
That the right wing hated.

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:06.258
-Yes. Yeah, what is that?
-Because it was socialism!

00:07:06.425 --> 00:07:09.095
-You know, my--
-But really, it was just
neighbors helping neighbors.

00:07:09.195 --> 00:07:10.229
And it really is.

00:07:10.462 --> 00:07:14.132
And you know, all of us,
if, right now,
something happened,

00:07:14.666 --> 00:07:17.403
all of us would band together
to help each other,

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:19.872
so why do we need to wait
till something happens?

00:07:19.972 --> 00:07:22.375
Well-- Or we'd band together
and eat each other.

00:07:22.608 --> 00:07:24.109
-I mean, you don't--
you don't know...
-That's true.

00:07:24.209 --> 00:07:26.445
-I mean...
-You know who'd be go--
they'd kill first.

00:07:26.545 --> 00:07:27.547
-(BILL LAUGHS)
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

